{"abstract":"BACKGROUND: Low-density lipoprotein cholesterol (LDL-C) is an important causal risk factor for atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease (ASCVD). However, a sizable proportion of middle-aged individuals with elevated LDL-C level have not developed coronary atherosclerosis as assessed by coronary artery calcification (CAC). Whether presence of CAC modifies the association of LDL-C with ASCVD risk is unknown. We evaluated the association of LDL-C with future ASCVD events in patients with and without CAC. METHODS: The study included 23 132 consecutive symptomatic patients evaluated for coronary artery disease using coronary computed tomography angiography (CTA) from the Western Denmark Heart Registry, a seminational, multicenter-based registry with longitudinal registration of patient and procedure data. We assessed the association of LDL-C level obtained before CTA with ASCVD (myocardial infarction and ischemic stroke) events occurring during follow-up stratified by CAC>0 versus CAC=0 using Cox regression models adjusted for baseline characteristics. Outcomes were identified through linkage among national registries covering all hospitals in Denmark. We replicated our results in the National Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute-funded Multi-Ethnic Study of Atherosclerosis. RESULTS: During a median follow-up of 4.3 years, 552 patients experienced a first ASCVD event. In the overall population, LDL-C (per 38.7 mg/dL increase) was associated with ASCVD events occurring during follow-up (adjusted hazard ratio [aHR], 1.14 [95% CI, 1.04-1.24]). When stratified by the presence or absence of baseline CAC, LDL-C was only associated with ASCVD in the 10 792/23 132 patients (47%) with CAC>0 (aHR, 1.18 [95% CI, 1.06-1.31]); no association was observed among the 12 340/23 132 patients (53%) with CAC=0 (aHR, 1.02 [95% CI, 0.87-1.18]). Similarly, a very high LDL-C level (>193 mg/dL) versus LDL-C <116 mg/dL was associated with ASCVD in patients with CAC>0 (aHR, 2.42 [95% CI, 1.59-3.67]) but not in those without CAC (aHR, 0.92 [0.48-1.79]). In patients with CAC=0, diabetes, current smoking, and low high-density lipoprotein cholesterol levels were associated with future ASCVD events. The principal findings were replicated in the Multi-Ethnic Study of Atherosclerosis. CONCLUSIONS: LDL-C appears to be almost exclusively associated with ASCVD events over ≈5 years of follow-up in middle-aged individuals with versus without evidence of coronary atherosclerosis. This information is valuable for individualized risk assessment among middle-aged people with or without coronary atherosclerosis.","author_count":13,"contributors":["Mortensen, Martin Bødtker","Dzaye, Omar","Bøtker, Hans Erik","Jensen, Jesper Møller","Maeng, Michael","Bentzon, Jacob Fog","Kanstrup, Helle","Sørensen, Henrik Toft","Leipsic, Jonathon","Blankstein, Ron","Nasir, Khurram","Blaha, Michael J","Nørgaard, Bjarne Linde"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-144585"],"filters":{"added":"2023-02","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"country":["Canada","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Harvard University","Houston Methodist Hospital","Johns Hopkins University","St. Pauls Hospital"],"keyword":["Arteries","Atherosclerosis","Calcium","Cardiovascular Diseases","Cardiovascular Diseases complications","Cholesterol LDL","Cohort Studies","Computed Tomography Angiography","Coronary Artery Disease diagnostic imaging","Coronary Vessels","Coronary artery disease","Denmark epidemiology","Epidemiology","Humans","Lipoproteins LDL","Middle Aged","Registries","Risk","Risk Assessment methods","Risk Factors","Vascular Calcification complications"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"low density lipoprotein cholesterol is predominantly associated with atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease events in patients with evidence of coronary atherosclerosis the western denmark heart registry","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1161/CIRCULATIONAHA.122.061010","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-144585","identifier":[{"id":"85151392506","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC10073288","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"36621817","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["au-1a93813a-f322-456f-b05e-c4e4c85a3fd5"],"keyword":["Atherosclerosis","Cardiovascular Diseases/complications","Cholesterol, LDL","Coronary Artery Disease/diagnostic imaging","Denmark/epidemiology","Humans","Middle Aged","Registries","Risk Assessment/methods","Risk Factors","Vascular Calcification/complications","arteries","calcium","cardiovascular diseases","cohort studies","computed tomography angiography","coronary artery disease","coronary vessels","epidemiology","lipoproteins, LDL","risk"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-D531C55F4D19F3829F0EB4BDC2E8A8CF"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1161/CIRCULATIONAHA.122.061010","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10073288/pdf/nihms-1860838.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - The Department of Cardiological Medicine B"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"us","key":"johns hopkins university","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Johns Hopkins University","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"ca","key":"st pauls hospital","loc":["Canada","America, North","OECD"],"name":"St. Pauls Hospital","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"us","key":"harvard university","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Harvard University","used":1},{"affno":"5","country":"us","key":"houston methodist hospital","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Houston Methodist Hospital","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Martin Bødtker","key":"mortensenpau","last":"Mortensen","locid":"f995af52-018b-4fa9-a398-c1bdc7ab1bd0","name":"Mortensen, Martin Bødtker","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Omar","key":"dzayepau","last":"Dzaye","name":"Dzaye, Omar","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Hans Erik","key":"bøtkerpau","last":"Bøtker","locid":"09b03c00-11a8-4f72-800f-181f26155750","name":"Bøtker, Hans Erik","orcid":"0000-0001-6358-8962","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jesper Møller","key":"jensenpau","last":"Jensen","locid":"a5cb9465-6652-4dda-806d-a8f33c29ff82","name":"Jensen, Jesper Møller","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Michael","key":"maengpau","last":"Maeng","locid":"e4bc8bdc-eeb6-419f-94dc-5eb69e03a564","name":"Maeng, Michael","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jacob Fog","key":"bentzenpau","last":"Bentzen","locid":"b19cdb97-a953-43ff-ad51-19d0a680caed","name":"Bentzon, Jacob Fog","name_org":"Bentzen, Jacob Fog","orcid":"0000-0002-3020-5002","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Helle","key":"kanstruppau","last":"Kanstrup","locid":"dd757af6-6cd3-4aad-a388-089d91c3f39e","name":"Kanstrup, Helle","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Henrik Toft","key":"sørensenpau","last":"Sørensen","locid":"4d49d93a-4913-4223-ba59-272e717a845d","name":"Sørensen, Henrik Toft","orcid":"0000-0003-4299-7040","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Jonathon","key":"leipsicpau","last":"Leipsic","name":"Leipsic, Jonathon","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Ron","key":"blanksteinpau","last":"Blankstein","name":"Blankstein, Ron","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"5","corresponding":null,"first":"Khurram","key":"nasirpau","last":"Nasir","name":"Nasir, Khurram","rank":11,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Michael J","key":"blahapau","last":"Blaha","name":"Blaha, Michael J","rank":12,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Bjarne Linde","key":"nørgaardpau","last":"Nørgaard","locid":"6bcdb292-78dc-4e8b-9fea-814d6a6852c9","name":"Nørgaard, Bjarne Linde","orcid":"0000-0002-4758-7203","rank":13,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["36621817"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1161/circulationaha.122.061010","issn":["00097322","15244539"],"issn_org":["00097322"],"issue":"14","journal":"Circulation","pages":"1053-1063","publisher":"Lippincott Williams and Wilkins","status":"p","title":"Circulation","volume":"147"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85151392506"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-1a93813a-f322-456f-b05e-c4e4c85a3fd5"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Low-Density Lipoprotein Cholesterol Is Predominantly Associated With Atherosclerotic Cardiovascular Disease Events in Patients With Evidence of Coronary Atherosclerosis : The Western Denmark Heart Registry","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-144585","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Functional MRI (fMRI) and EEG may reveal residual consciousness in patients with disorders of consciousness (DoC), as reflected by a rapidly expanding literature on chronic DoC. However, acute DoC is rarely investigated, although identifying residual consciousness is key to clinical decision-making in the intensive care unit (ICU). Therefore, the objective of the prospective, observational, tertiary centre cohort, diagnostic phase IIb study ‘Consciousness in neurocritical care cohort study using EEG and fMRI’ (CONNECT-ME, NCT02644265) was to assess the accuracy of fMRI and EEG to identify residual consciousness in acute DoC in the ICU. Between April 2016 and November 2020, 87 acute DoC patients with traumatic or non-traumatic brain injury were examined with repeated clinical assessments, fMRI and EEG. Resting-state EEG and EEG with external stimulations were evaluated by visual analysis, spectral band analysis and a Support Vector Machine (SVM) consciousness classifier. In addition, within- and between-network resting-state connectivity for canonical resting-state fMRI networks was assessed. Next, we used EEG and fMRI data at study enrolment in two different machine-learning algorithms (Random Forest and SVM with a linear kernel) to distinguish patients in a minimally conscious state or better (≥MCS) from those in coma or unresponsive wakefulness state (≤UWS) at time of study enrolment and at ICU discharge (or before death). Prediction performances were assessed with area under the curve (AUC). Of 87 DoC patients (mean age, 50.0 ± 18 years, 43% female), 51 (59%) were ≤UWS and 36 (41%) were ≥ MCS at study enrolment. Thirty-one (36%) patients died in the ICU, including 28 who had life-sustaining therapy withdrawn. EEG and fMRI predicted consciousness levels at study enrolment and ICU discharge, with maximum AUCs of 0.79 (95% CI 0.77–0.80) and 0.71 (95% CI 0.77–0.80), respectively. Models based on combined EEG and fMRI features predicted consciousness levels at study enrolment and ICU discharge with maximum AUCs of 0.78 (95% CI 0.71–0.86) and 0.83 (95% CI 0.75–0.89), respectively, with improved positive predictive value and sensitivity. Overall, both machine-learning algorithms (SVM and Random Forest) performed equally well. In conclusion, we suggest that acute DoC prediction models in the ICU be based on a combination of fMRI and EEG features, regardless of the machine-learning algorithm used.","author_count":30,"contributors":["Amiri, Moshgan","Fisher, Patrick M.","Raimondo, Federico","Sidaros, Annette","Hribljan, Melita Cacic","Othman, Marwan H.","Zibrandtsen, Ivan","Albrechtsen, Simon S.","Bergdal, Ove","Hansen, Adam Espe","Hassager, Christian","Højgaard, Joan Lilja S.","Jakobsen, Elisabeth Waldemar","Jensen, Helene Ravnholt","Møller, Jacob","Nersesjan, Vardan","Nikolic, Miki","Olsen, Markus Harboe","Sigurdsson, Sigurdur Thor","Sitt, Jacobo D.","Sølling, Christine","Welling, Karen Lise","Willumsen, Lisette M.","Hauerberg, John","Larsen, Vibeke Andrée","Fabricius, Martin","Knudsen, Gitte Moos","Kjaergaard, Jesper","Møller, Kirsten","Kondziella, Daniel"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-702291"],"filters":{"added":"2023-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["France","Germany"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Heinrich Heine University Düsseldorf","Jülich Research Centre","Sorbonne University"],"keyword":["Acute brain injury","Disorders of consciousness","EEG","Functional MRI","Machine learning"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"multimodal prediction of residual consciousness in the intensive care unit the connect me study","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"acces