i":"10.1007/978-3-030-91625-1_11","eisbn":["9783030916251"],"isbn":["9783030916244"],"issn":["03029743"],"pages":"192-211","publisher":"Springer","series":"Lecture Notes in Computer Science","status":"p","subtitle":"26th Nordic Conference, NordSec 2021, Virtual Event, November 29–30, 2021, Proceedings","title":"Secure IT Systems"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85119856071"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-16cec89e-036d-435c-a30e-f4b9ffcaf90e"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University"],"title":"Preliminary Security Analysis, Formalisation, and Verification of OpenTitan Secure Boot Code","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-056753","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Aims: To evaluate 1) the relative use of automated external defibrillators (AEDs) at different types of AED locations 2) the percentage of AEDs crossing location types during OHCA before use 3) the AED coverage distance at different types of AED locations, and 4) the 30-day-survival in different subgroups. Methods: From 2014–2018, AEDs used by bystanders during out-of-hospital cardiac arrest (OHCA) in the Region of Southern Denmark were collected. Data regarding registered AEDs was retrieved from the national AED-network. The OHCA site and AED placement was categorized into; 1) Residential; 2) Public; 3) Nursing home, 4) Company/workplace; 5) Institution; 6) Health clinic and 7) Sports facility/recreational. To evaluate 30-day-survival, groups 4–7 were pooled into one Mixed group. Results: In total 509 OHCAs were included. There was high relative usage of AEDs from public places, nursing homes, health clinics and sports facilities, and low relative usage from companies/workplaces, residential areas and institutions. Of AEDs used during residential OHCAs 39% were collected from public places. AEDs placed in residential areas and public places had a coverage of 575 m (IQR 130–1300) and 270 m (IQR5-550), respectively. Thirty-day- survival in public, residential and mixed groups were 49%, 14% and 67%, respectively. Conclusion: The relative use of AEDs from public places, nursing homes, sports facilities and health clinics was high, and AEDs used during OHCA in residential areas were most frequently collected from public places. AEDs placed in both residential areas and public places may have a wider coverage area than proposed in current literature.","author_count":9,"contributors":["Sarkisian, Laura","Mickley, Hans","Schakow, Henrik","Gerke, Oke","Starck, Simon Michael","Jensen, Jonas Junghans","Møller, Jacob Eifer","Jørgensen, Gitte","Henriksen, Finn Lund"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-640407"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Sarkisian, Laura"],"keyword":["AED","Bystander","Coverage","Defibrillation","Defibrillator","OHCA","Out of hospital cardiac arrest"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-SDR"],"OT":["Emergency Medical Services, Region of Southern Denmark, Damhaven 12, 7100 Vejle"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-SDR","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"use and coverage of automated external defibrillators according to location in out of hospital cardiac arrest","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/180053688/RESUS_D_20_01370_R1_til_PURE.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-640407","identifier":[{"id":"85101772431","type":"scopus"},{"id":"33581227","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["sdu-eaa06a47-ce21-4ba1-8d26-91b491b4253e"],"keyword":["AED","Automated external","Bystander","Coverage","Defibrillation","Defibrillator","OHCA","Out-of-hospital cardiac arrest"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-BCF3DA7C55B91AE9018A23118277B554"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/180053688/RESUS_D_20_01370_R1_til_PURE.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Clinical Research, KI, OUH, Research Unit OPEN (Odense)","SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Clinical Research, KI, OUH, Research unit of Cardiology (Odense)"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"miscellaneous","l2":"DI-OT","l2n":"Miscellaneous","l3":"DI-OT","l3nl":"Other Research","l3nn":"Miscellaneous","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Miscellaneous","namevar":["Emergency Medical Services, Region of Southern Denmark, Damhaven 12, 7100 Vejle"],"nora_code":"DI-OT","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"dk","key":"region of southern denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-SDR","l3nl":"Region of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"Region of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Region of Southern Denmark","namevar":["Odense University Hospital"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-SDR","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Laura","key":"sarkisianpau","last":"Sarkisian","locid":"472392ba-c3e3-4a00-ae8e-9a24b7706ab3","name":"Sarkisian, Laura","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Hans","key":"mickleypau","last":"Mickley","locid":"f6789f9a-e23b-4ab8-bd7d-a3c168107cdb","name":"Mickley, Hans","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Henrik","key":"schakowpau","last":"Schakow","name":"Schakow, Henrik","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Oke","key":"gerkepau","last":"Gerke","locid":"596baeb1-6754-4d53-948f-30470dd1d976","name":"Gerke, Oke","orcid":"0000-0001-6335-3303","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Simon Michael","key":"starckpau","last":"Starck","name":"Starck, Simon Michael","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Jonas Junghans","key":"jensenpau","last":"Jensen","name":"Jensen, Jonas Junghans","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jacob Eifer","key":"møllerpau","last":"Møller","locid":"5e4d3cb6-b0a0-4541-aea1-30e73f7c10b8","name":"Møller, Jacob Eifer","orcid":"0000-0003-2873-5845","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Gitte","key":"jørgensenpau","last":"Jørgensen","name":"Jørgensen, Gitte","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Finn Lund","key":"henriksenpau","last":"Henriksen","locid":"df341fee-5044-4b19-b725-cfbd0e7914bf","name":"Henriksen, Finn Lund","orcid":"0000-0002-1192-532X","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["33581227"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.resuscitation.2021.01.040","issn":["03009572","18731570"],"issn_org":["03009572"],"journal":"Resuscitation","pages":"112-119","publisher":"Elsevier","status":"p","title":"Resuscitation","volume":"162"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85101772431"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Miscellaneous","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Miscellaneous","Other Research","Region of Southern Denmark","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-eaa06a47-ce21-4ba1-8d26-91b491b4253e"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Use and Coverage of Automated External Defibrillators According to Location in Out-of-Hospital Cardiac Arrest","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-640407","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Background: Mechanical circulatory support (MCS) with either extracorporeal membrane oxygenation or Impella has shown potential as a salvage therapy for patients with refractory out-of-hospital cardiac arrest (OHCA). The objective of this study was to describe the gradual implementation, survival and adherence to the national consensus with respect to use of MCS for OHCA in Denmark, and to identify factors associated with outcome. Methods: This retrospective, observational cohort study included patients receiving MCS for OHCA at all tertiary cardiac arrest centers (n = 4) in Denmark between July 2011 and December 2020. Logistic regression and Kaplan–Meier survival analysis were used to determine association with outcome. Outcome was presented as survival to hospital discharge with good neurological outcome, 30-day survival and predictors of 30-day mortality. Results: A total of 259 patients were included in the study. Thirty-day survival was 26%. Sixty-five (25%) survived to hospital discharge and a good neurological outcome (Glasgow–Pittsburgh Cerebral Performance Categories 1–2) was observed in 94% of these patients. Strict adherence to the national consensus showed a 30-day survival rate of 30% compared with 22% in patients violating one or more criteria. Adding criteria to the national consensus such as signs of life during cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR), pre-hospital low-flow < 100 min, pH > 6.8 and lactate < 15 mmol/L increased the survival rate to 48%, but would exclude 58% of the survivors from the current cohort. Logistic regression identified asystole (RR 1.36, 95% CI 1.18–1.57), pulseless electrical activity (RR 1.20, 95% CI 1.03–1.41), initial pH < 6.8 (RR 1.28, 95% CI 1.12–1.46) and lactate levels > 15 mmol/L (RR 1.16, 95% CI 1.16–1.53) as factors associated with increased risk of 30-day mortality. Patients presenting signs of life during CPR had reduced risk of 30-day mortality (RR 0.63, 95% CI 0.52–0.76). Conclusions: A high survival rate with a good neurological outcome was observed in this Danish population of patients treated with MCS for OHCA. Stringent patient selection for MCS may produce higher survival rates but potentially withholds life-saving treatment in a significant proportion of survivors.","author_count":21,"contributors":["Mørk, Sivagowry Rasalingam","Stengaard, Carsten","Linde, Louise","Møller, Jacob Eifer","Jensen, Lisette Okkels","Schmidt, Henrik","Riber, Lars","Andreasen, Jo Bønding","Thomassen, Sisse Anette","Laugesen, Helle","Freeman, Phillip Michael","Christensen, Steffen","Greisen, Jacob Raben","Tang, Mariann","Møller-Sørensen, Peter Hasse","Holmvang, Lene","Gregers, Emilie","Kjaergaard, Jesper","Hassager, Christian","Eiskjær, Hans","Terkelsen, Christian Juhl"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-685262"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Mørk, Sivagowry Rasalingam"],"keyword":["Cardiopulmonary resuscitation","Extracorporeal Membrane Oxygenation","Impella","Mechanical circulatory support","Out of hospital cardiac arrest"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-NP","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-NDR"],"NP":["DI-NP-ALL","DI-NP-HJER"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-NDR","DI-NP-HJER","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"mechanical circulatory support for refractory out of hospital cardiac arrest a danish nationwide multicenter study","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s13054-021-03606-5","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["This work was supported by the Danish Heart Foundation [20-R142-A9498-22178]; and Health Research Foundation of Central Denmark Region [R64-A3178-B1349]; and Danish Helicopter Emergency Medical Service Research Fund; and Aase og Ejnar Danielsens Fond; and Snedkermester Sophus Jacobsen og hustru Astrid Jacobsens Fond; and Henry og Astrid Møllers Fond; and a research grant from Aarhus University to Dr. Mørk; and Lundbeck Foundation [R186-2015–2132] to Dr. Hassager. None of the mentioned sources listed participated in design of the study, study collection, analysis, interpretation of data, or writing of the manuscript. Dr. Stengaard reports speaker’s fees from Rosche Diagnostics, outside the submitted work. Dr. Møller reports personal fees and grants from Orion Pharma, Novartis, Astra Zeneca, Abbott and Abiomed and served at scientific advisory board for Boehringer Ingelheim, outside the submitted work. Dr. Freeman reports grants from St. Jude and Astra Zeneca and personal fees from Meril Lifesciences and Edwards Lifesciences, outside the submitted work. Dr. Hassager reports grants from the Lundbeck Foundation and speaker’s honoraria from Abiomed, outside the submitted work. Dr. Kjaergaard reports non-financial participation in the advisory board for the CoCa Trial. Dr. Terkelsen is supported by an unrestricted research grant from the Danish Heart Foundation. The remaining authors have no conflicts of interest to declare."],"id":"S01-000.00-685262","identifier":[{"id":"85106734803","type":"scopus"},{"id":"34022934","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC8141159","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["sdu-9291f87d-08ce-443c-9d50-fcc04691824d"],"keyword":["Cardiopulmonary resuscitation","Extracorporeal membrane oxygenation","Impella","Mechanical circulatory support","Out-of-hospital cardiac arrest"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-3-7-1A74226EF126D0BA3D5001AFF6DE20CD"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/183275792/s13054_021_03606_5.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/1