{"abstract":"OBJECTIVE: The DanGer Shock trial test the hypothesis that left ventricular (LV) mechanical circulatory support with Impella CP transvalvular microaxial flow pump improves survival in patients with ST segment elevation acute myocardial infarction complicated by cardiogenic shock (AMICS) compared to conventional guideline-driven treatment. This paper describes the rationale and design of the randomized trial, in addition to the baseline characteristics of the population screened and enrolled so far. METHODS: The DanGer Shock study is a prospective, multicenter, open-label trial in patients with AMICS randomized 1:1 to Impella CP or current guideline-driven therapy with planned enrollment of 360 patients. Patients comatose after out of hospital cardiac arrest are excluded. Eligible patients are randomized immediately following shock diagnosis. Among patients randomized to receive Impella CP, the device is placed prior to angioplasty. The primary endpoint is all-cause mortality at 180 days. Baseline characteristics of patients screened and randomized in the DanGer Shock as of June 2018 are compared with 2 contemporary AMICS studies. RESULTS: As of end of June 2018, 314 patients were screened and 100 patients were randomized. Patients had median arterial lactate of 5.5 mmol/L (interquartile range 3.7-8.8 mmol/L), median systolic blood pressure of 76 mmHg (interquartile range 70-88 mmHg), and median LV ejection fraction of 20% (interquartile range 10%-30%). CONCLUSION: The DanGer Shock trial will be the first adequately powered randomized trial to address whether mechanical circulatory LV support with Impella CP can improve survival in AMICS. Baseline characteristics of the first 100 randomized patients indicate a population in profound cardiogenic shock.","author_count":15,"contributors":["Udesen, Nanna Junker","Møller, Jacob Eifer","Lindholm, Matias Greve","Eiskjær, Hans","Schäfer, Andreas","Werner, Nikos","Holmvang, Lene","Terkelsen, Christian Juhl","Jensen, Lisette Okkels","Junker, Anders","Schmidt, Henrik","Wachtell, Kristian","Thiele, Holger","Engstrøm, Thomas","Hassager, Christian"],"cuid":["S03-100.00-023375"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":3,"continent":["Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"corpau":["DanGer Shock investigators"],"corresponding_author":["Møller, Jacob Eifer"],"country":["Germany","Norway"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Bonn University Hospital","Hannover Medical School","Leipzig University","Oslo University Hospital","Universitaetsklinikum Bonn - I. Medizinische Klinik","University of Oslo"],"keyword":["Aged","Blood Pressure","Cause of Death","Female","Guideline Adherence","Heart Assist Devices","Humans","Lactic Acid blood","Male","Middle Aged","Patient Selection","Percutaneous Coronary Intervention","Prospective Studies","Research Design","ST Elevation Myocardial Infarction blood","Shock Cardiogenic blood","Stroke Volume","Time Factors"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-SDR"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-SDR","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"rationale and design of danger shock danish german cardiogenic shock trial","src":["au","ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ahj.2019.04.019","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["This study is an investigator-initiated trial with a lead principal investigator, JEM and a steering committee responsible for the scientific content of the protocol and the conduct of the study. The trial was initially approved by the Danish National Ethical Committee in November 2012 (H-2-2012-034) and registered at ClinicalTrials.gov ( NCT01633502 ). In January 2013, the first patient was enrolled and the study was initially planned to end in 2018 including only Danish patients (DANSHOCK). The clinical landscape in Denmark is unique as 4 specialized invasive centers (Rigshospitalet Copenhagen, Odense, Skejby, and Aalborg) provide primary PCI service for the entire country. Hence, almost 3000 patients are annually triaged by wireless transmission of ECG recording and a short history from the ambulance and referred to acute angiography for suspected STEMI with minimal time delay. However, despite this, enrollment rate has been prolonged with only 100 patients randomized until end of June 2018. Slow enrollment is mainly due to lower than the anticipated incidence rate of shock, according to the inclusion criteria and a higher rate of exclusion (described in detail in the discussion section). This led to a change in study composition in 2018. To increase the enrollment rate, additional German sites with high volume of PCI procedures and experience with mechanical circulatory support were invited to participate in the study. German centers are anticipated to start enrollment at the beginning of 2019. The project has received funding from the Danish Heart Association and in 2018 the manufacturer of the Impella device, Abiomed Europe GmbH (Aachen, Germany), provided a research grant allowing for expansion of the study to Germany (DanGer shock). The funders have no influence on the design of the study, data analysis, data interpretation or drafting of manuscripts. The trial will be monitored by an independent Data Safety Monitoring Board."],"id":"S03-100.00-023375","identifier":[{"id":"85066251828","type":"scopus"},{"id":"000475295200008","type":"wos"},{"id":"31176289","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["au-92ab3d63-6b96-4b9c-8075-3dab7423c6eb","ku-0b7bad6d-7012-4129-91cf-39370c0a3751","sdu-925e2ce9-cc52-49fd-88e6-9c1652deff06"],"keyword":["Aged","Blood Pressure","Cause of Death","Female","Guideline Adherence","Heart-Assist Devices","Humans","Lactic Acid/blood","Male","Middle Aged","Patient Selection","Percutaneous Coronary Intervention","Prospective Studies","Research Design","ST Elevation Myocardial Infarction/blood","Shock, Cardiogenic/blood","Stroke Volume","Time Factors"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-6-3E468827ED1D6BED66793C1B1BF85B52"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.au.dk/ws/files/196442423/1_s2.0_S0002870319301085_main.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ahj.2019.04.019","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/151167460/Rationale_and_design_of_DanGer_shock.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/237417348/1_s2.0_S0002870319301085_main.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.au.dk/ws/files/196442446/1_s2.0_S0002870319301085_main.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2019","yearSub":"2019"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Clinical Research, KI, OUH, Research unit of Cardiology (Odense)"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"region of southern denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-SDR","l3nl":"Region of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"Region of Southern Denma