","DI-PR-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Other Companies","Private Research","Technical University of Denmark","Universities","bk medical aps"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-cb698814-1279-4ee5-adbd-f686e222cad0"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark"],"title":"Recent advances in blood flow vector velocity imaging","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-365312","year":"2011","yearsub":"2011"}
{"abstract":"Background- End-stage heart failure is associated with impaired cardiac output (CO) and organ blood flow. We determined whether CO and peripheral perfusion are maintained during exercise in patients with an axial-flow left ventricular assist device (LVAD) and whether an increase in LVAD pump speed with work rate would increase organ blood flow. Methods and Results- Invasively determined CO and leg blood flow and Doppler-determined cerebral perfusion were measured during 2 incremental cycle exercise tests on the same day in 8 patients provided with a HeartMate II LVAD. In random order, patients exercised both with a constant (˜9775 rpm) and with an increasing pump speed (+400 rpm per exercise stage). At 60 W, the elevation in CO was more pronounced with increased pump speed (8.7±0.6 versus 8.1±1.1 L · min(-1); mean±SD; P=0.05), but at maximal exercise increases in CO (from 7.0±0.9 to 13.6±2.5 L · min(-1); P","author_count":12,"contributors":["Brassard, Patrice","Jensen, Annette S","Nordsborg, Nikolai Baastrup","Gustafsson, Finn","Møller, Jacob Eifer","Hassager, Christian","Boesgaard, Søren","Hansen, Peter Bo","Olsen, Peter Skov","Sander, Kåre","Secher, Niels H.","Madsen, Per Lav"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-519520"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"central and peripheral blood flow during exercise with a continuous flow left ventricular assist device constant versus increasing pump speed a pilot study","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1161/CIRCHEARTFAILURE.110.958041","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-519520","identifier":[{"id":"84856379721","type":"scopus"},{"id":"21